Treat \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) as small-signal ground. Since the source, gate, and body of \(M_2\) are at AC ground, \(M_2\) simply presents \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to ground. Thus \(M_1\) is the common-gate device whose source is degenerated by \(r_{o2}\).

Apply a test voltage \(v_t\) at the output and let the node-\(X\) voltage be \(v_x\). KCL at \(X\) gives

\[
\frac{v_x}{r_{o2}}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})v_x+\frac{v_x-v_t}{r_{o1}}=0,
\]

where \(g_{mb1}\) accounts for the body effect of \(M_1\), since its body is at \(V_{DD}\) while its source is at \(X\). The test current is

\[
i_t=\frac{v_x}{r_{o2}}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
= r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
R_{\text{out}}
= r_{o1}+\left[1+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}\right]r_{o2}.
\]

If body effect is neglected, or the body of \(M_1\) is tied to its source,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
= r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

for \(g_{m1}r_{o1},g_{m1}r_{o2}\gg1\).
